hings  so i'm going to talk a bit about the  case and then  i'm also going to talk about some some  real basics that we want to keep in mind  when we're thinking about  stoicism or really any intentional way  of living  within the workplace and then there's a  number of what we can call stoic  points that i'm going to lead us through  so i  the first of these is whether as stoics  we should expect other people  in the workplace to buy into stoicism or  not  and what the implications for that are  the second has to do  with truth the stoics believe that human  beings have an innate  desire for truth and value truth  and there was an appeal to truth as  something like a transcendent value in  this  workplace case so we're going to look at  what stoics actually have to say  about truth then we're also going to  talk about something else that the  stoics thought was  absolutely central to human beings our  social  nature we are social beings there are a  number of implications  coming out of that for how we ought to  live work think in the workplace  we're also going to talk about the  workplace as you might say  a field of things that the stoics called  externals or  indifference and whether that means that  we should not care about them  or whether that means that we need to be  careful  in how we're dealing with these matters  and then  this the second to last thing i want to  talk about is something that i think we  all need to keep in mind  every once in a while some of us need to  be reminded of it a little bit more  which is that we're not the sage we  are uh the the procoptone or the  proficience if we're using greek or  latin or you could say the stoic in  training and by aspiration that means  that we're constantly  trying to work on ourselves and that  means we're going to screw up and so we  should think about well what should we  do  when we screw up what should we do when  we make mistakes  finally i do want to address the issue  that that we absolutely have to bring up  what if you're in a workplace that's  actually bad  in some respects should you as a stoic  say well  i'll just put up with it this is really  a great opportunity for me to  train my stoicism or should you say well  this is actually exploitative or this is  abusive and at a certain point  i should not have to put up with this so  we're going to talk about each of these  points  i'm not going to try to cover everything  in the depth or detail that it deserves  obviously each of these is something we  could do a much longer talk about  but hopefully in the q a and discussion  we can explore this a bit more  the case that i have in mind to use here  as you might say examples of how not to  behave  as a stoic pertains to a  firing of an employee whose name was  samuel jackson  he worked for the little corporation as  apparently a communications  specialist and he got fired because  he engaged in some stereotyping and it's  not clear exactly when he did this it  may he may have said this out loud he  may have put this into an email  and um he said some some bad things  about  asians as a entire class  i mean it's very there's a little bit of  a side note if you think about  asians it's not a nationality it  encompasses everybody from the indian  subcontinent  way up into you know north east asia  and so this is you know quite a  statement for him to make  i'm not going to repeat the statement  because i don't want to engage in  stereotyping like that he was called  out for that and he was told he needed  to apologize  and he gave what the the corporation  thought was an insufficient apology and  he was  fired now notice he's a communications  person  so we have a role here in which you'd  expect that this person would be  cognizant of you know the niceties of  social conventions and how to  communicate effectively  and what not to say as well so  now it goes before courts and  uh here's here's where it gets  particularly interesting  he claimed stoicism as a pretext for  what he had done in the workplace and  why it was okay for him to do it  in the article in in hr review it says  stoic beliefs meant that he was bound to  say what he liked  without fear of offending other people  and and  he clarifies that what he wanted to say  was what he thought to be the truth  and without any repercussions so he's  making the case that what stoicism  really amounts to  is such a great love for truth that you  should say  whatever is on your mind if you think  that it's true without caring about  how it's going to affect anyone else  consequences in terms of  how they might you know view you  consequences in terms of how those words  might affect them and so  under the equality act of 2000 in  britain  stoicism is now being considered  a protected belief now  that's kind of interesting because it  means that there will be other people  making similar  arguments and using stoicism as a  pretext for similar behavior in the  workplace  in the court case he actually said that  the realization that the consequences of  what i say would cause offense  would not stop me from saying it so here  we're posed with a really  interesting case did he get stoicism  right  is he in a certain respect abusing  stoicism  by claiming its mantle in order to avoid  having to take any responsibility for  his actions  these are questions that we can think  out a further one that we can think out  if we've decided that he actually got  things wrong is  well what can we learn from this example  of  how not to behave in the workplace let's  start with talking about some real  basics and this goes beyond stoicism as  such and would extend to any sort of  virtue ethics tradition or wisdom  tradition or any  intentional way of living i think  there's there's  four main things that we want to keep in  mind when we're talking  about the workplace and how we're  applying  a philosophy or way of life to it  so the first is how we should behave  towards others how should we treat other  people  in the workplace so others is a key  dimension  and then we can also talk about  ourselves how do we evaluate  ourselves and how do we develop  ourselves  further into being you know better and  better people because that's that's what  anything that counts as a virtue ethics  ought to be  guiding us towards becoming better  people not just being complacent with  who we  currently are and then there's two other  vectors that we can talk about we can  talk about the work  itself and our roles and our duties that  come into play with that  and then we can talk about the  overarching situation  of the workplace itself which can be  understood  as a domain of all sorts of personal  interactions we can also look at it as  something like a community it could be a  very dysfunctional community  it could be a very well coordinated one  and so these four dimensions i think are  good to keep in mind  when you look at places where people are  discussing stoicism  one of the beginner mistakes that people  bring up quite often without meaning to  necessarily  is making the assumption that because  they've decided that they want to be a  stoic  and they want to start practicing  stoicism everybody else should be held  to  that as well everybody else should be  viewed as if they're stoic  so they'll say you know things like well  you shouldn't be affected by the things  that i'm saying because it's only  your judgment that makes it hurtful to  you  that is a very unstoic way to behave for  several reasons first of all it's  completely imprudent  prudence is one of the key virtues of  stoicism  and to think that somehow because one  has changed oneself a little bit  that everyone else in the world is going  to change that's complete foolishness  the opposite of prudence it's also  unjust it's placing a burden on them  that they don't actually deserve to have  and you know it there's there's other  things that we can say about it as well  but one of the problems with this case  is  i think and i'm sort of reading this  into this case  it sounds like samuel jackson is  assuming that his co-workers  really shouldn't be offended by his  using ethnic stereotypes to  characterize those who belong to that  that whole set of ethnicities really  or anybody else who might say well  that's the wrong thing to be doing in  the workplace  i find that offensive because it's  actually an unjust  action that you're engaging in and so  it's it's  you know this is i think something  really worth keeping in mind  if you're going to try to be a stoic in  the workplace  do not expect other people to suddenly  change their mentalities  and become stoic along with you  epictetus actually tells us tells us in  the end caridian  if you're going to start being a stoic  start being a philosopher keep it to  yourself for the time being  until you've managed to work on yourself  for a while  samuel jackson explained his actions by  saying that according to him stoicism  meant  that he had a duty or a right  to speak the truth as he saw it  regardless of whether it would offend  anybody or any other consequences  and he's partly right in that stoicism  does  value truth truth is is held to be  something that is a good for human being  something that we have an  innate desire for this is part of what  allows stoics to say that there's  something like a  natural law that would say that we  should actually cultivate wisdom  it doesn't say that we have an automatic  right or duty to speak our mind anytime  and as a matter of fact one of the  things if you actually are a stoic  that you should hold about truth is is  that you're probably  wrong when it comes to a lot of the  things that you think  are true ethnic stereotypes are a great  example of this  claiming that that one knows something  about an entire  swath of people and can say things about  them  i mean that should set off alarm bells  for anybody who claims to be a stoic  that's sort of like ding ding ding  wow i i have some really screwed up  preconceptions  right i have some really screwed up  judgments i have some really  screwed up opinions and i'd better start  working on these  and so this provides us with a good  place to talk about what would an  actual stoic say about this well  you know the stoics actually talked  about disciplines  and epitidis as everyone knows talks  about the discipline of desire the  discipline of action or choice and the  discipline of ascent  that entire discipline of ascent is  about how to get our ideas  right and straight in our heads seneca  talks about disciplines  in a slightly different way in letter 89  he says that that ethics is divided into  three branches  the first assigns to each thing it's  proper value and determines what it's  worth  the second deals with impulse the third  deals with actions right  the objective of ethics are to enable  you to judge what each thing is worth to  enable you to  entertain a well adjusted and controlled  impulse and the third to enable you to  achieve harmony between your impulse and  action so you can be consistent  these would seem to say that this guy  actually is not a stoic he's  he's using stoicism he's actually  untruthful  about what stoicism consists in  another key aspect of this and there's  much more that could be said about this  is that as epictetus tells us  sticking with ideas stubbornly doubling  down on them  does not actually make you a stoic or  even rational  he gives us an example somebody who made  a really stupid decision  and then pig-headedly stuck with it  against  any sort of you know argument or counter  evidence and epictetus says  the resolve to stick with something  against you know  say any outside interference that's  only good if your ideas are actually  true  clearly in this case sticking with an  ethnic stereotype and then doubling down  by refusing to apologize  properly about it and then going to  court and claiming that this  is done because of stoicism that i think  would fit  under what epictetus is talking about  another key dimension of stoic ethics is  that we  as human beings are social beings we  are not meant to be totally isolated  from each other or to push  others away as a matter of fact we're  only really following the stoic path  when we can get along with other people  there are going to be obstacles  especially when other people have  wrong-headed ideas but we should make  our  best efforts to get along so there's  this pro-social  nature and this is something that the  stoics think that we ought to work on  and expand and there's a lot of  different ways in which this can  happen we can talk about some of that in  the q  a one key element of this is that  justice is a virtue for  stoics and justice doesn't just mean  giving everybody what they deserve or  honoring commitments that we make  it also involves as you know cicero  seneca  marcus aurelius epictetus make crystal  clear  beneficence or kindness  being good to other people not doing  things that are going to be hurtful to  them in fact  doing the opposite so saying offensive  things to other people  is really going against this this  key teaching of stoicism and it's going  against the  virtue of justice another key aspect of  this  particularly important in the workplace  is understanding our roles stoic ethics  tells us  that a good portion of our duties come  from our roles and these might even be  included in our job description  for a person in communications you would  think that  communicating effectively with other  people and then being able to handle  being told that you've got something  wrong and coursing course adjusting  would be part of that and this can apply  to all of us we all have different roles  as epictetus reminds us the role of  socrates is not the role  of diogenes is not the role of xeno  socrates is supposed to go around and  ask people questions diogenes is  supposed to go around as a cynic and be  kind of a jerk but also teach people  things  xeno was a teacher right so each of us  has our workplace roles and we're  supposed to be living  up to them as best as we can another key  thing that i think can come out of stoic  philosophy and this is  really there in marcus aurelius who  reminds himself about this over and over  again but also in epictetus  is we can conceive of ourselves while  still remaining individuals as  parts of larger holes  in which we have our part to play and we  only  work well within that context by  harmonizing with  as best we can what other people are  doing and then i think we can also think  about  having proper emotional comportments  towards our fellow workers we shouldn't  be contemptuous of them  we shouldn't try to you know say things  that are going to be offensive to them  here i want to read a little passage  from marcus he says fit yourself into  accord with the things in which your  portion has been cast  and then he says and love the people  among whom your lot has fallen  but love them truly be affectionate  be kind be good to other people within  the workplace  that is how a stoic would approach  dealing with matters in their their  relations with other co-workers with  customers with  uh so you know supervisors subordinates  all the people who we have to engage  with we should have the proper  components towards them one of the  challenges for applying stoicism within  the workplace  is looking at all the things that the  workplace comprises  and not jumping to the conclusion  that none of it really matters from a  stoic perspective as a matter of fact  it does matter even though most of what  we're dealing with  do fall into what stoics call the  externals  or the indifference the indifference are  things that  don't make us either truly happy or  truly miserable because they don't by  themselves affect  our moral goodness or moral badness but  they can certainly play  a major role in it because what we do  with the indifference how we deal with  them  and how we make decisions in  prioritizing about them and  also the work that we're engaged with  that  does have an impact on us so we  can exercise the virtues of for example  courage or fortitude or temperance or  self-control  in the way that we're dealing with  things in the workplace do we stick with  our work  and diligently get through it or do we  slough off and  you know say things like well nobody's  going to notice if i cut corners over  here  that by itself may bear on something  that is indifferent  but it has to do with our character  and likewise the things that we say in  in some respects may be by themselves  indifferent but they  they have an effect on us so it's very  important to keep in mind that just  because  we say as stoics that things like  your your the amount of money that  you're getting paid  or the number of hours that you're  putting in or the boxes that you fill or  whatever it happens to be that those  things are strictly speaking  indifference they can be preferred  or rejected and the use that we make of  them  is absolutely essential in whether we're  going to become  more virtuous or more vicious  one of the things that we need to bear  in mind as i mentioned a little bit  earlier  is that if we're going to be stoics we  cannot conceive of stoicism as something  like an on off switch you picked up the  book  and now you're a stoic or you began  practicing and now you're a stoic  stoicism you might say is a continuum  and it's not even one where you  necessarily are going to make steady  progress you're going to screw up you're  going to fail  you're going to fall you're going to  make mistakes you're going to think that  you've got things right and then  eventually  re-read something and get reminded that  you've actually got some things  off you're mistaken about some things  and then what do we do in a  in a case like that do we double down  stubbornly  on saying that we'